<p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
    <h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I Expect My Future to Be... – Lesson # 3</h2>
    <p style="text-align: center; font-style: italic;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Future Plans and Goals</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Expect My Future to Be...</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Discuss a recent learning experience. Interactive: Engage in discussions about academic and future professional interests.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 0 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can explain a future learning experience by asking and responding to questions (e.g., "What will you be doing five years from now?" "I will be working as an engineer"). Can ask and answer questions about recent academic progress (e.g., "What did you learn this trimester?" "I improved my math skills"). Can explain academic goals by describing learning experiences in discussions (e.g., "I plan to take extra science classes next year").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h3 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: left;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h3>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 0 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a "Future Vision Board" with images representing various career paths (e.g., engineer, doctor, artist, business owner), educational achievements (e.g., university diploma, learning new skills), and personal development (e.g., traveling). Students work in small groups and orally describe what they see in the pictures and which images resonate with their personal future aspirations. They share a few sentences like, "I see someone achieving their goals," or "I want to have a successful career like that."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 0 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models sentences about future plans using the target grammar and vocabulary. "In five years, I will be working as a teacher." "I have to plan my lessons carefully." "I want to achieve my professional goals." The teacher also explicitly models the pronunciation of future tense structures ("I will study hard," "I will be preparing for exams") and modals ("I have to be successful"). Special attention is given to the minimal pairs /θ/ in "think" and /s/ in "sink," by contrasting words and having students repeat. For example, "I *think* about my future," vs. "I don't *sink* in despair."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 0 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of "future plans" and the difference between 'want to' and 'have to'.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'I will be studying,' am I talking about yesterday, now, or tomorrow?" (Tomorrow/Future)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'I have to send the application,' is it optional or necessary?" (Necessary)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a 'goal' something you have already done, or something you want to achieve?" (Something you want to achieve)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 0 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Future Plans and Goals")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher plays an audio recording of two 8th-grade students, María and Carlos, discussing their academic and future career interests. The dialogue, presented also on a projected slide, incorporates the target grammar (modals of necessity, WH-questions, future continuous) and vocabulary (future, goals, plan, business, career, opportunity, vision, development, ambitious, important, future-oriented). The register is informal, simulating a real conversation between friends. For example:</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 0 0; font-style: italic;">María: "What are your academic goals for next year, Carlos? I hear you want to improve your math skills."<br>Carlos: "Yes, I have to study harder. I plan to take extra science classes, if possible. In five years, I will be preparing for university applications. My vision is to have a career in business."<br>María: "That's ambitious! You must work hard. I want to achieve my goals in art. What do you think you will be doing for a career after university?"<br>Carlos: "I will be looking for opportunities to develop my own business. It's an important goal for me."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 0 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Engage in discussions about academic and future professional interests.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 0 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen and then answer gist questions orally in pairs, then share with the class.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is Carlos's main academic goal for next year?" (To study harder and take extra science classes)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What kind of career does Carlos want?" (A career in business, possibly developing his own business)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What are María's goals?" (Goals in art, achieving them)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 0 0;">(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Speaking: Modals of necessity ("have to," "must"), WH-questions for educational/career goals, Future continuous. Vocabulary: future, goals, plan, business, career, opportunity, vision, development, plan, achieve, prepare, set, have to, ambitious, important, future-oriented, if, when, for, in, ambitiously, carefully.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 0 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – Sentence Completion (Oral):</b> The teacher provides sentence stems on the board or a worksheet. In pairs, students orally complete the sentences, ensuring correct use of target grammar. They take turns saying the full sentence.</li>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"In five years, I ____ (be / work) as a ____."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"To achieve my goals, I ____ (have to / study) ____."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"You ____ (must / prepare) your ____ carefully."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I want a good ____, I ____ (will be / learn) new skills."</li>
                    </ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice – Future Scenario Prompt Cards:</b> The teacher distributes prompt cards with various future scenarios (e.g., "be a chef," "travel the world," "start a business," "learn a new language," "get a university degree"). Students work in pairs. Partner A picks a card and creates a sentence using "In [X] years, I will be [verb+ing]..." or "I want to [verb]..." Partner B asks a follow-up WH-question (e.g., "What will you be cooking?" "Where will you be traveling?"). They swap roles. Encourage the use of vocabulary like "opportunity," "vision," "ambitious," and "development."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction – Academic Progress Questions:</b> Students engage in a short Q&A with their partner about recent academic progress and immediate goals. They use provided sentence starters and target grammar. Peer correction is encouraged.</li>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did you learn this trimester that you found important?" (e.g., "I improved my math skills.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What do you plan to study more carefully next trimester?" (e.g., "I plan to take extra science classes next year.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do you have to study for any specific exams soon?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 0 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Role-play: "Future Achievers Interview." Students work in pairs to conduct an interview about their "Future Plans and Goals." One student acts as an interviewer for a "Future Leaders" podcast, and the other is the "future achiever."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 0 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students receive role cards with prompts (e.g., "Ask about their biggest career goal," "Ask what they will be doing in 10 years," "Ask what they have to do to achieve their vision," "Offer encouragement").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Interviewer must ask at least 5 WH-questions, using future continuous and modals of necessity. Achiever must respond with full sentences, incorporating target vocabulary (career, business, opportunity, development, ambitious, plan, achieve, important).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Encourage the use of pragmatic functions such as discussing goals, responding to questions, and offering encouragement ("You have to work hard to achieve your goals!"). Students should try to speak ambitiously and carefully.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pairs perform their interviews in front of another pair or the teacher.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Example questions/statements:</b> "What will you be doing five years from now?" "What are your academic goals for next year?" "If you want to start your own business, what steps do you have to take?" "You must set clear goals for your future development!"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 0 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on the speaking performance during the "Future Achievers Interview." Peers also provide feedback using a simple checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 0 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each pair performs their "Future Achievers Interview" in front of another pair or the teacher.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 0 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of future tense structures and modals (e.g., "I will study hard," "I have to be the best").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (future, goals, plan, business, career, opportunity, vision, development, ambitious, important).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of modals of necessity ("have to," "must").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct formation and use of future continuous ("What will you be doing?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to ask and respond to WH-questions about academic and career goals.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Engagement in collaborative goal-setting discussion, offering suggestions and feedback (Sociolinguistic competence).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 0 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences on a slip of paper before leaving.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word I used today to talk about my future is ______."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Talking about my future plans today was ______ (easy/a bit difficult) because ______."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new thing I learned about my classmates' future goals or their vision today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can I use 'have to' or 'will be doing' to talk about my own goals outside of class (e.g., for homework, chores, personal development)?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 0 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b> The speaking activities provided ample opportunity for students to practice future tenses, modals, and relevant vocabulary. Most students engaged well in pair work and were able to discuss their future plans using the target structures. Some students still need more practice with the pronunciation of the future continuous and differentiating /θ/ and /s/ sounds. I will need to provide more scaffolded sentence frames for students who struggled with forming WH-questions. For next lesson, I will connect the oral discussion of goals to written expression, focusing on sentence construction and paragraph writing about future plans and academic goals.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 0 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This speaking lesson provided the oral foundation for discussing future plans. In Lesson 4 (Writing), students will transition from speaking about their future goals to writing descriptive paragraphs or short essays about their academic and professional ambitions, integrating the same vocabulary and grammatical structures.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>